It uses hazeljs (https://github.com/hazelgrove/hazeljs, MIT), loaded from npm, and frames Hazel's own editor
(https://hazel.org, MIT) from a branch build at build.hazel.org.

The hexagrams' order (King Wen) and names are traditional. The names here are short glosses of our own and common
renderings of the Chinese titles.

The Tarot's woodcut faces (tarot/woodcut, a git submodule, not a copy) are Woodcut tarot, https://github.com/SONDLecT/woodcut-tarot,
by way of our fork, https://github.com/matthewhammer/woodcut-tarot, at commit e017dd5c8e: dedicated to the public domain
under CC0 1.0, and so not under this repository's licences. Its 78 cards are traced from the Jean Noblet tarot (Paris,
c. 1650; Bibliotheque nationale de France, RESERVE KH-34), with the Six to Ten of Wands from the Jacques Vieville tarot
(Paris, c. 1650), from the BnF's scans on Gallica; the colour set's small red ovals are the BnF's ownership stamps.

The astrology oracle (astrology/, work in progress) writes its rules in Hazel. Its chart's formulas (the Julian day,
sidereal time, the obliquity of the ecliptic, the ascendant and the midheaven) follow Jean Meeus, Astronomical
Algorithms (2nd ed., Willmann-Bell, 1998), rewritten in Hazel. Its check, node/astrology.mjs, compares them with
Astronomy Engine (https://github.com/cosinekitty/astronomy, MIT, Don Cross), a development dependency the pages do not
load. Its outer planets (Jupiter to Neptune) use Paul Schlyter's orbital elements and perturbations, from "How to compute
planetary positions" (https://stjarnhimlen.se/comp/ppcomp.html), rewritten in Hazel. Its sky modules otherwise rewrite
in Hazel the positions of the Sun, Moon and planets from astronomia
(https://github.com/commenthol/astronomia, MIT, Copyright (c) 2013 Sonia Keys, (c) 2016 commenthol), a port of Meeus: its
solar.js, moonposition.js, planetelements.js and pluto.js; the tables are generated from its source by
tools/make-sky-tables.py. Its glyphs (astrology/glyphs.svg) are drawn here, under this repository's licences.
Its constellation figures (astrology/constellations.svg, made by tools/make-constellations.mjs) are drawn here from
ConstellationLines by Marc van der Sluys (https://github.com/MarcvdSluys/ConstellationLines, Copyright (c) 2005-2026,
CC BY 4.0), which says which stars to join, and the Yale Bright Star Catalogue, 5th revised edition (Hoffleit and
Warren, 1991; CDS catalogue V/50), for where the stars are and how bright: projected, sized and styled here, under CC BY 4.0.

The pages link to other sites for fuller meanings (Cafe au Soul for the I Ching; Biddy Tarot, by Brigit Esselmont,
and Labyrinthos, by Tina Gong, for the Tarot's cards). Those pages and their text are their publishers'; nothing of
theirs is copied here.
